# Object Pooling for Game Entities

## Context
- When developing games with frequent object creation/destruction (bullets, enemies, particles)
- When experiencing frame rate drops during intense gameplay moments
- When garbage collection pauses are noticeable during gameplay

## Requirements
- Use Phaser's built-in Group and GroupConfig for object pooling
- Pre-allocate objects for frequently created entities
- Properly reset object state when recycling from the pool
- Implement custom pools when Phaser's built-in options are insufficient

## Examples

<example>
```typescript
// Using Phaser's built-in Group for pooling bullets
export class BulletManager {
  private bulletPool: Phaser.GameObjects.Group;
  
  constructor(scene: Phaser.Scene) {
    // Create a bullet pool with Phaser's Group
    this.bulletPool = scene.add.group({
      classType: Bullet,
      maxSize: 30,
      runChildUpdate: true,
      createCallback: (bullet: Bullet) => {
        bullet.setActive(false);
        bullet.setVisible(false);
      }
    });
  }
  
  fireBullet(x: number, y: number, direction: number): Bullet | null {
    // Get a bullet from the pool
    const bullet = this.bulletPool.get(x, y) as Bullet;
    
    // No bullets left in pool
    if (!bullet) return null;
    
    // Reset and initialize the bullet
    bullet.fire(x, y, direction);
    return bullet;
  }
  
  returnBullet(bullet: Bullet): void {
    // Return bullet to pool
    bullet.reset();
    this.bulletPool.killAndHide(bullet);
  }
}

// The bullet class
class Bullet extends Phaser.GameObjects.Sprite {
  private speed: number = 300;
  
  constructor(scene: Phaser.Scene, x: number, y: number) {
    super(scene, x, y, 'bullet');
    this.setActive(false);
    this.setVisible(false);
  }
  
  fire(x: number, y: number, direction: number): void {
    this.setPosition(x, y);
    this.setActive(true);
    this.setVisible(true);
    
    // Set velocity based on direction
    this.scene.physics.velocityFromAngle(direction, this.speed, this.body.velocity);
  }
  
  reset(): void {
    this.setActive(false);
    this.setVisible(false);
    this.body.reset(0, 0);
  }
  
  update(time: number, delta: number): void {
    // Check if bullet is out of bounds
    if (this.x < -50 || this.x > this.scene.game.config.width + 50 ||
        this.y < -50 || this.y > this.scene.game.config.height + 50) {
      this.reset();
      this.scene.events.emit('bulletKilled', this);
    }
  }
}
```
Using Phaser's Group for object pooling leverages the engine's built-in recycling system, providing efficient performance while keeping code clean. The bullet pool pre-allocates objects and reuses them instead of creating new instances.
</example>

<example type="invalid">
```typescript
// Creating new objects every time without pooling
export class BadBulletManager {
  private scene: Phaser.Scene;
  
  constructor(scene: Phaser.Scene) {
    this.scene = scene;
  }
  
  fireBullet(x: number, y: number, direction: number): void {
    // Creates a new bullet instance every time
    const bullet = new Bullet(this.scene, x, y);
    bullet.fire(direction);
    
    // No recycling mechanism, objects are left for garbage collection
    this.scene.time.delayedCall(3000, () => {
      bullet.destroy();
    });
  }
}
```
This approach creates a new bullet object every time one is fired, leading to frequent garbage collection pauses that harm performance, especially during intense gameplay when many bullets are created in a short time.
</example>

## Phaser-Specific Notes

1. Phaser provides several built-in pooling mechanisms:
   - `Phaser.GameObjects.Group` with `maxSize` for basic pooling
   - `Phaser.GameObjects.Group.createMultiple()` for pre-populating pools
   - `Phaser.Actions` for manipulating multiple objects efficiently
   
2. For particle effects, use Phaser's Particle Emitter Manager which handles pooling internally:
   ```typescript
   const particles = scene.add.particles('flare');
   const emitter = particles.createEmitter({
     maxParticles: 30,
     lifespan: 2000,
     // Other configuration
   });
   ```

3. When using pools with physics objects, remember to properly reset physics bodies:
   ```typescript
   reset() {
     this.body.reset(0, 0);
     this.setVelocity(0, 0);
     this.setAngularVelocity(0);
     this.setAcceleration(0, 0);
   }
   ``` 